% ============================================================================
% past-tense-ed-sound — regular past-tense verbs whose final -ed ending is
% pronounced with a different SOUND depending on the sound before it
% (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% The ELEVENTH literacy sub-skill library in this loop's sweep, and a
% genuinely new phonics pattern beyond CCSS RF.K.2's five parts and the
% spelling/whole-text/vocabulary slices already shipped: the regular -ed
% past-tense suffix is spelled the SAME way every time, but PRONOUNCED one
% of three different ways depending on the final sound of the base verb.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? past_tense_ed_sound(walked, $Sound)
%       % t_sound
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `past_tense_ed_sound(word, sound)` carrying the source citation,
% so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine
% returns the sound WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls,
% and abstains on any past-tense word not one of these three source-cited
% examples. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a sound and recall which
% example word carries it:
%
%     ? past_tense_ed_sound($W, id_sound)
%       % wanted
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from 7ESL's "Pronunciation of ED: Past Tense
% Pronunciation for Regular Verbs" article, which states the three voicing
% rules and gives worked base-verb → past-tense examples for each:
%
%     word      sound      rule (verbatim)                                                          example (verbatim)
%     -------   --------   -----------------------------------------------------------------------  -------------------
%     walked    t_sound    "Final -ed is pronounced /t/ after all voiceless sounds."                 "Walk → Walked"
%     lived     d_sound    "Final -ed is pronounced /d/ after voiced sounds."                         "Live → Lived"
%     wanted    id_sound   "Final -ed is pronounced /id/ after 'T', and 'D' sounds. The sound /id/    "Want → Wanted"
%                          adds a whole syllable to a word."
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — a general
% ESL-learning site, not a primary government or research-institution
% source, the same tier this stdlib already reserves for its other
% non-.gov language sources (e.g. Wikipedia's Greek-alphabet and
% Morse-code entries). A past-tense word not one of these three — e.g.
% `played` (also pronounced with the /d/ sound, but not one of the three
% example words this source's own worked examples name) — is deliberately
% NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a sound.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table past_tense_ed_sound {
    columns word, sound

    row (walked, t_sound)
    row (lived, d_sound)
    row (wanted, id_sound)

    source "Final -ed is pronounced /t/ after all voiceless sounds."
    locator "https://7esl.com/pronunciation-of-ed/"
    trust consensus
}
